WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:05.199
"Beauty is only skin deep." So says a well-known proverb in English.

2
00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:18.468
It expresses the sentiment that personality, intellect, and spirit—the qualities beneath the skin—are as important as, or more important than, physical appearance when judging a person's worth.

3
00:00:18.469 --> 00:00:25.793
But what about people who go to great lengths to change their bodies, treating their skin like an artist's canvas?

4
00:00:25.794 --> 00:00:34.543
Would they agree that appearance is less important than character? Many who tattoo, pierce, and scar their bodies see no contradiction.

5
00:00:34.544 --> 00:00:40.835
For them, body art, as these practices are known, is an expression of inner beauty.

6
00:00:40.836 --> 00:00:50.663
The practice of decorating the body, with ink, metal, or wood, goes back to the days of ancient Egypt and perhaps earlier, to Mesopotamian times.

7
00:00:50.664 --> 00:00:57.044
The form of body art most widespread through history is the simple painting or coloring of the skin.

8
00:00:57.045 --> 00:01:04.604
Ancient Egyptians used kohl, a powder made from soot and other ingredients, to darken and decorate the eyes.

9
00:01:04.605 --> 00:01:11.198
Some groups of Native Americans painted their bodies and faces with colored clay before going into battle.

10
00:01:11.199 --> 00:01:22.351
Ancient cultures in northern Africa and India stained the skin in intricate patterns by grinding the leaves of the henna plant and applying the paste with a fine brush.

11
00:01:22.352 --> 00:01:33.778
These forms of decoration may have had practical origins; kohl protected the eyes from the sun's glare, and face paint helped warriors identify fellow members of their tribe.

12
00:01:33.779 --> 00:01:38.333
But the decorations were also intended to change the wearer's appearance.

13
00:01:38.334 --> 00:01:45.876
Kohl enhanced the shape of a woman's eyes, making her more attractive—just as mascara and eyeliner do today.

14
00:01:45.877 --> 00:01:52.097
War paint made a warrior's face look grotesque or horrific in order to intimidate the enemy.

15
00:01:52.098 --> 00:01:56.707
Frequently, ancient cosmetics also played a ceremonial role.

16
00:01:56.708 --> 00:02:12.141
Egyptian priests and pharaohs wore kohl when performing religious rites, Native Americans painted their faces for tribal ceremonies, and women in almost every culture where the plant was known applied henna tattoos for their weddings.

17
00:02:12.142 --> 00:02:24.528
Most people nowadays do not consider tattoos, scars, or piercings to have any religious significance, but cultures the world over have decorated their bodies to reflect spiritual beliefs.

18
00:02:24.529 --> 00:02:33.115
In sacred ceremonies, Australian Aborigines made shallow cuts on the torsos of both boys and girls when they reached adolescence.

19
00:02:33.116 --> 00:02:40.673
When the cuts healed, the scars showed that the child had gone through the rites necessary to become an active member of the tribe.

20
00:02:40.674 --> 00:02:45.718
The scars also indicated the boy or girl's relationship with the spirit world.

21
00:02:45.719 --> 00:02:57.595
Scarification, as this practice is called, was once widespread in Australia, but as a ritual practice, it is now restricted mainly to a remote northeastern territory.

22
00:02:57.596 --> 00:03:07.915
In the Sepik region of Papua New Guinea, the skin of adolescent boys is sliced with a bamboo sliver to test the boys' self-discipline and initiate them into manhood.

23
00:03:07.916 --> 00:03:12.213
These people believe that the human race was created by crocodiles.

24
00:03:12.214 --> 00:03:23.080
The slicing creates a pattern of scars like the teeth marks of a crocodile, symbolizing that the boy was swallowed by the ancestral crocodile and reborn as a man.

25
00:03:23.081 --> 00:03:30.628
In other cultures, body art had no spiritual dimension, but was merely a way to display social status.

26
00:03:30.629 --> 00:03:35.745
Priests of the ancient Aztec empire wore jewelry in piercings in their lower lips.

27
00:03:35.746 --> 00:03:46.002
The jewelry was made of silver, gold, and precious stones and was considered a sign of power; the larger the lip ornament, the greater the priest's power.

28
00:03:46.003 --> 00:03:56.942
Humans have pierced many parts of the body for jewelry: the ears, of course, and the nose (through either the septum or the nostrils) can be ornamented with studs or rings.

29
00:03:56.943 --> 00:04:07.104
In countless cultures, the size and value of the jewelry—whether of bone or precious metal, plain or adorned with gems—displayed the wealth and status of the wearer.

30
00:04:07.105 --> 00:04:14.216
In many cases, the amount of pain endured during piercing or scarification also conveyed the wearer's status.

31
00:04:14.217 --> 00:04:22.200
Tattooing is achieved by different techniques around the world, but all of them require breaking the skin with a thin, sharp object.

32
00:04:22.201 --> 00:04:29.239
In Myanmar, a brass tool that resembles a pen is used to slit the skin; then pigment is rubbed over the wound.

33
00:04:29.240 --> 00:04:40.354
Eskimos, as well as other Arctic and Subarctic tribes, attach a thread dipped in pigment to a needle, which then inserts the dye as the needle breaks the skin.

34
00:04:40.355 --> 00:04:47.573
The peoples of Polynesia and Micronesia use a small, rakelike tool to prick pigment into the skin.

35
00:04:47.574 --> 00:04:56.740
In modern, sterile tattoo parlors, pain is still part of the process. Today's sophisticated tattoo guns still pierce the skin to inject the ink.

36
00:04:56.741 --> 00:05:07.163
A precise group of needles attached to a bar is driven into the skin 80 to 150 times per second, injecting a small amount of ink each time.

37
00:05:07.164 --> 00:05:13.105
Even today, people are most impressed by the largest, and therefore most painful, tattoos.

38
00:05:13.106 --> 00:05:21.145
These traditions may seem harsh, but even more extreme forms of body art, collectively known as "body shaping," have been practiced.

39
00:05:21.146 --> 00:05:30.639
For almost 1,500 years, the feet of young girls in China were tightly bound so that their feet would stay tiny as the girls grew.

40
00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:42.344
Foot binding resulted in broken and deformed feet that made walking difficult and painful, but the small "lotus feet," as they were called, were considered beautiful and were highly sought after.

41
00:05:42.345 --> 00:05:46.574
The Padaung women of Myanmar extend their necks with brass rings.

42
00:05:46.575 --> 00:05:54.455
The large, shiny rings are first placed around a girl's neck at the age of four or five, and more rings are added as she ages.

43
00:05:54.456 --> 00:06:00.274
The rings put pressure on the collarbone and shoulders, and make the neck appear an unusual length.

44
00:06:00.275 --> 00:06:07.510
If the rings are removed, the woman's weakened neck may not be able to support her head, forcing her to remain lying down.

45
00:06:07.511 --> 00:06:16.996
Nonetheless, the Padaung consider long necks an important element of female beauty. Body shaping is not restricted to ancient or isolated peoples.

46
00:06:16.997 --> 00:06:23.711
The corsets worn by European women in the nineteenth century changed the shape of their ribs, sometimes breaking them.

47
00:06:23.712 --> 00:06:31.868
Made of cloth and strips of whalebone, and laced exceedingly tight, corsets forced women's bodies into a "perfect" hourglass figure.

48
00:06:31.869 --> 00:06:36.477
The many forms of body art practiced through time have seen a modern comeback.

49
00:06:36.478 --> 00:06:44.310
The availability of surgical steel and clean, sterile methods, which reduce the risk of infection, have made piercings more popular.

50
00:06:44.311 --> 00:06:49.956
Today, a piercing in the nose, navel, lip, or eyebrow is no more surprising than an earring.

51
00:06:49.957 --> 00:06:57.393
Tattooing was once acceptable only for men in certain segments of society: sailors, soldiers, convicts, and gang members.

52
00:06:57.394 --> 00:07:08.282
Nowadays, society's attitude to tattoos has largely changed, and tattoos are a popular and acceptable method of personal or artistic expression, for both men and women.

53
00:07:08.283 --> 00:07:17.990
Among the growing body-art subculture, there are women who once again wear corsets with the aim of changing their body shape, and men who undergo scarification.

54
00:07:17.991 --> 00:07:30.436
Whether it be a teenage girl receiving a henna tattoo for the prom or a body artist displaying rows of rings in his lips and nose, body art is both a very ancient and very modern tradition.

